  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "SIM-kurim mostly shows that this scenario cannot be answered at the parent envelope layer alone. It is strong on non-canonical evolution and boundary clarity around grid([pcid,payload]), unknown-pCID, and signature-placement questions, but it does not define ring membership, hop-by-hop authorization, refusal records, or reconfiguration behavior when Bob cannot forward to Carol.",
    "strengths": [
      "Strong evolution discipline: the parent seed plus explicit variant matrix avoids premature wire-format lock-in.",
      "Very clear layer boundaries keep envelope questions separate from transport-family behavior.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling keeps sparse-knowledge and partial-understanding pressure visible."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No ring semantics, membership model, or definition of forwarding authorization.",
      "No peer-local refusal record or audit trail for Bob, Carol, or later auditors.",
      "The parent sim is an owner/inventory artifact, not a concrete specimen with scenario behavior."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "It could be misread as transport-family evidence when it is mostly envelope-level scaffolding.",
      "Unresolved outer-pCID reveal and signature-binding choices may conflict with hop-by-hop authorization and privacy needs."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Where is Bob's refusal recorded so Carol or an auditor can verify it later?",
      "What minimal outer metadata must be visible for hop-by-hop authorization without central authority?",
      "On refusal, does the higher-layer transport break the ring, skip the hop, or reconfigure membership, and how does the envelope expose that?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
